132 0 obj
<<
/AP 644 0 R
/DA (/TimesNewRoman 12 Tf 0 g)
/DS (font: 'Times New Roman',serif 12.0pt; text-align:left; color:#000000 )
/F 4
/FT /Tx
/Ff 41947136
/MK 645 0 R
/MaxLen 1500
/P 18 0 R
/RV (<?xml version="1.0"?><body xfa:APIVersion="Acroform:2.7.0.0" xfa:spec="2.1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/"><p dir="ltr" style="margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12pt"><span style="xfa-spacerun:yes">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160; </span>This paper proposes an effective technique for detecting sleep apnea syndrome by using ECG signals. In this method, the Pan-Tompkins algorithm is applied for the detection of P, Q, R, S and T points, and the 13 parameters obtained from the signal morphology are used to perform the classification. Threshold analysis is applied to identify apnea episodes, being a non-invasive and safe method, but also useful in identifying apnea on a large number of analyzed parameters. This method is based on comparing each parameter with a predetermined threshold and counting the parameters that exceed its value. Finally, if a signal has at least 5 parameters exceeding the threshold values, the signal will indicate the presence of apnea. So, the results obtained demonstrate that the algorithm achieves an accuracy of 84% on a set of 50 analyzed samples, highlighting the efficiency of the proposed method. From the perspective of the contributions made, it can be highlighted the efficiency of the apnea syndrome detection process by developing an analysis based on specific parameters and comparing them with certain thresholds established according to the characteristics of the evaluated signal.</p></body>)
/Rect [57.7845 156.19 559.595 358.043]
/Subtype /Widget
/T (Abstract)
/TU <>
/Type /Annot
/V (          This paper proposes an effective technique for detecting sleep apnea syndrome by using ECG signals. In this method, the Pan-Tompkins algorithm is applied for the detection of P, Q, R, S and T points, and the 13 parameters obtained from the signal morphology are used to perform the classification. Threshold analysis is applied to identify apnea episodes, being a non-invasive and safe method, but also useful in identifying apnea on a large number of analyzed parameters. This method is based on comparing each parameter with a predetermined threshold and counting the parameters that exceed its value. Finally, if a signal has at least 5 parameters exceeding the threshold values, the signal will indicate the presence of apnea. So, the results obtained demonstrate that the algorithm achieves an accuracy of 84% on a set of 50 analyzed samples, highlighting the efficiency of the proposed method. From the perspective of the contributions made, it can be highlighted the efficiency of the apnea syndrome detection process by developing an analysis based on specific parameters and comparing them with certain thresholds established according to the characteristics of the evaluated signal.)
>>
endobj
